1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,030
It is July eighteen ninety-seven.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,650
You are twenty-two, standing on a
noisy Seattle dock,

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:09,850
staring at a wooden steamship
named the Portland.

4
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:14,390
The morning headline on every street
corner reads like a fairy tale:

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,870
a ton of solid gold just arrived
from the far north.

6
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:22,350
That wide-eyed boy on the pier was me.

7
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:26,710
My name is Walt Becker, and I
thought I would be a

8
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:27,930
rich man before winter.

9
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,630
My friend Frank and I spent our
life savings on two boat

10
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:36,930
tickets up the coast to the tiny
muddy port of Dyea,

11
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:37,290
Alaska.

12
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:40,210
When our steamer dropped anchor in August,

13
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:42,610
there were no wharves.

14
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:46,170
We had to haul our wooden crates
through ice-cold salt water at

15
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:46,670
low tide.

16
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,130
Thousands of men were stranded on the
beach among piles of bacon,

17
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,910
flour, and rusty picks, realizing the
real trail had not even begun.

18
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,850
We bought a broken-down mule
for eighty dollars,

19
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:05,530
packed our sacks, and marched into the dark
rainforest toward the mountains.

20
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:11,070
The trail was a knee-deep bog of black
mud and sharp boulders.

21
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:16,030
Within three days, our poor pack
animal collapsed and died.

22
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:18,510
Frank and I took up our straps,

23
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:21,330
carrying seventy pounds each on our backs.

24
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:25,210
But we had no idea how much
weight lay ahead.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,890
By late September, the first
frosts had come,

26
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:31,990
and we reached the foot of the
deadly Chilkoot Pass.

27
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,730
Later that winter, the North-West Mounted
Police established a strict post at

28
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:40,950
the summit, commanded by
Superintendent Sam Steele.

29
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,850
The Mounties had seen starving
prospectors before,

30
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:49,090
and they made a hard law that saved
thousands of lives that

31
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:49,410
winter.

32
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:54,590
No man was allowed into Canada unless
he brought one full ton

33
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:59,370
of supplies—enough food and gear to
survive one full year.

34
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:02,750
That meant hundreds of pounds of flour,

35
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,970
bacon and dried beans, plus tents,

36
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,650
stoves, and dozens of tools.

37
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:12,250
You could not carry two thousand
pounds in one trip.

38
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:15,650
You had to shuttle fifty-pound
loads up the mountain,

39
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:17,330
forty times over.

40
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:20,710
A man would walk five miles
up with a pack,

41
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:25,090
dump it in the snow, and walk five
miles back down for

42
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:25,770
the next load.

43
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:29,710
By the time you moved your whole
outfit across the border,

44
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:34,050
you had walked over four hundred miles
on the same short trail.

45
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:39,790
As November brought fierce blizzards, the
prices for basic supplies doubled at

46
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:42,030
the staging camp called The Scales.

47
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:46,530
Prices at the camps climbed with
every mile up the trail.

48
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:50,810
If your money ran out, you worked
as a packer for others

49
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:52,250
or went home.

50
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,390
Frank and I had twenty dollars
left between us.

51
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,110
If we could not scale the great
white wall before us,

52
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:02,750
we would starve in the snow.

53
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:08,390
In December eighteen ninety-seven, we
reached the base of the final climb:

54
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:10,990
the terrifying Golden Stairs.

55
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:14,870
It was a brutally steep wall
of snow and ice,

56
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,430
the last thousand feet climbing
into the blinding mist.

57
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:23,050
Men chopped fifteen hundred steps
directly into the snow.

58
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:28,050
A single file line of thousands
stretched from bottom to top.

59
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,770
You could not stop or rest.

60
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:33,830
If you stepped out of line to
catch your breath,

61
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,750
you might wait three hours to get back in.

62
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:42,250
Every man carried a seventy-pound canvas
sack bound to his forehead and

63
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:43,730
shoulders by leather straps.

64
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:48,290
The wind at the top blew so
hard it could knock a

65
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:51,110
grown man off his feet into
the gulch below.

66
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:55,930
We buried our flour sacks in deep snow
trenches at the summit,

67
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,370
marking our stash with long wooden sticks.

68
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:04,110
Then we slid back down the
mountain on our boots in six

69
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:08,410
minutes, only to begin the six-hour
climb all over again.

70
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:13,410
On April third, eighteen
ninety-eight, disaster struck.

71
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:17,670
A massive avalanche roared down the
slopes near Sheep Camp.

72
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,870
Over sixty men and women were buried
under thirty feet of snow.

73
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:26,390
We spent two sleepless days digging
with wooden shovels.

74
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:29,410
We saved a few, but dozens perished.

75
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:33,510
It was a dark reminder that the
Klondike took its price before

76
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:34,550
you ever saw gold.

77
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:38,730
If you were standing on that frozen
mountain with the long trail

78
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:43,470
to Lake Bennett still ahead, would you
have turned back or kept

79
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:43,930
going?

80
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:46,430
Frank and I pushed on.

81
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:51,690
By May, all four thousand pounds
of our gear sat on the

82
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:53,370
icy shore of Lake Bennett.

83
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:56,930
The frozen lake stretched more
than twenty miles,

84
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:00,250
and thousands of stampeders
camped along it,

85
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,730
waiting for the spring ice to break.

86
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:04,950
There were no steamers here.

87
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:09,150
Every man had to cut down pine
trees and build his own

88
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,390
boat from raw timber.

89
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,870
We dug a deep saw-pit.

90
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:17,030
I stood inside the pit covered
in blinding sawdust,

91
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,090
while Frank pulled the long
blade from above.

92
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:25,450
It took ten exhausting days to saw
three hundred pine planks by

93
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:28,330
hand, our palms blistered and bleeding.

94
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:32,890
We caulked the seams with old wool
rags soaked in hot pine

95
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:35,750
tar to make our wooden scow watertight.

96
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:38,330
We named her The Lucky Duck,

97
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:42,890
painted the name with soot, and loaded
our precious supplies aboard.

98
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:55,490
On May twenty-ninth, eighteen ninety-eight, the
ice on Lake Bennett cracked with

99
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:56,510
a roar like thunder.

100
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:56,510
Over seven thousand handmade wooden boats
pushed off into the water at

101
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:57,950
the exact same hour.

102
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,390
It was an armada of fools,

103
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:05,430
floating down five hundred miles of
unknown rivers toward the Yukon.

104
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:10,570
But between Lake Bennett and Dawson City
lay the most treacherous waters

105
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:11,610
in the Northern territory.

106
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:16,910
In June, the river narrowed into a
roaring white wall of foam:

107
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:26,436
Miles Canyon and the White Horse Rapids.

108
00:06:26,286 --> 00:06:26,484
The canyon was barely a hundred feet wide,

109
00:06:26,334 --> 00:06:28,910
framed by towering basalt cliffs that
squeezed the wild Yukon River.

110
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:32,170
Waves smashed against the rock walls,

111
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:37,010
creating whirlpools capable of sucking a
fully loaded barge to the bottom.

112
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:39,890
In the first days after the ice broke,

113
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:42,610
about one hundred and fifty
boats were wrecked,

114
00:06:42,460 --> 00:06:44,090
and several men drowned.

115
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:49,770
Superintendent Steele stepped in again, stopping
all boats at the canyon entrance.

116
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:54,630
No boat was allowed through unless guided
by an experienced river pilot,

117
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:58,390
while women and children were required
to walk around the canyon on

118
00:06:58,240 --> 00:06:58,650
foot.

119
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:04,250
Experienced local pilots charged twenty-five
dollars per trip to steer heavy boats

120
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:06,330
safely through the roaring rapids.

121
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:10,450
Frank and I managed to hire a licensed
pilot with our remaining

122
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:10,850
cash.

123
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,670
We gripped the wooden gunwales,
clenched our teeth,

124
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:17,230
and plunged into the roaring gorge.

125
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:20,710
The current seized us like a wooden toy.

126
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:26,210
Water splashed over our flour barrels as
we narrowly dodged a submerged

127
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:26,730
rock.

128
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:31,350
For two terrifying minutes, the
river roared in our ears,

129
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:35,590
until finally the canyon opened
into calm water.

130
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:39,190
We were drenched, exhausted, but alive.

131
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:47,589
Our boat was whole, and the golden
city still lay more than

132
00:07:47,439 --> 00:07:47,930
four hundred miles downstream.

133
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:52,870
On June sixteenth, eighteen ninety-eight,
our boat rounded the bend of the

134
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,550
Yukon River and docked at Dawson City.

135
00:07:56,220 --> 00:08:00,250
Two years earlier, Dawson had been
little more than a fishing camp

136
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:01,230
on the river flats.

137
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:05,870
Now it was a bursting boomtown of
tens of thousands.

138
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:13,430
Wooden saloons, gambling halls, and canvas
tents lined dirt streets turned into

139
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,330
deep, foul-smelling mud.

140
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:18,310
The prices in Dawson were staggering.

141
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:22,590
Fresh food cost many times what
it did in Seattle,

142
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:25,850
and men paid for everything in gold dust.

143
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:29,010
A single fresh apple could cost a dollar,

144
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:32,690
bought by men still weak and
bleeding at the gums from the

145
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:33,610
scurvy of the winter.

146
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:37,710
Frank and I grabbed our shovels and
rushed out to Bonanza Creek

147
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:40,470
and Eldorado Creek, eager to
stake our claim.

148
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:43,090
But when we arrived at the creeks,

149
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:46,830
we faced a crushing truth that hit us
harder than the winter

150
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:47,210
cold.

151
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:52,870
Every rich foot of gold-bearing creek had
already been staked by the

152
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:58,110
old-timers back in eighteen
ninety-six and ninety-seven.

153
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:01,350
The rich claims were owned by a
handful of men called the

154
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:05,790
Klondike Kings, who were pulling thousands
of dollars from the dirt daily.

155
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:13,070
Out of one hundred thousand people who
left their homes for the

156
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,650
Klondike, only thirty to forty thousand
ever reached Dawson City.

157
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:19,530
Of those, only four thousand ever
found any gold at all.

158
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:22,890
And fewer than several hundred
became wealthy.

159
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:29,270
Most stampeders worked for wages, digging
gravel and hauling wood just to

160
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:30,530
pay for their ticket home.

161
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:35,330
Frank worked in a sawmill, while I spent
three months hauling timber

162
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:39,630
for saloon floors until we had enough
cash for steamship passage.

163
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:45,450
In August eighteen ninety-nine, news arrived
that gold was discovered on the

164
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:47,910
sandy beaches of Nome, Alaska.

165
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:52,150
Within a single week, thousands of
people left Dawson City,

166
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:54,430
chasing the next golden dream.

167
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:57,110
Frank and I did not follow them.

168
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:00,130
We sold our tent, packed our
woolen blankets,

169
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:02,630
and boarded a boat back down the river.

170
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:06,630
We returned home to Seattle with no
gold in our pockets,

171
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:09,710
but with stories that would
last us a lifetime.

172
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:13,350
If you have stayed with me this
far by the fire,

173
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:18,470
leave a like and subscribe; that
is how I know you want

174
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:20,130
more stories like this one.

175
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,270
I never found gold on the Yukon,

176
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:27,050
but whenever the winter wind
howls outside my cabin,

177
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:31,390
I am twenty-two again, standing
on top of the world.
